Note: this repo's ddgs-based path happened to survive this sandbox's IP
reputation on this run; SearXNG's own duckduckgo engine (a different scraper,
html.duckduckgo.com rather than ddgs's impersonation-based backend) did NOT,
per section 3 above. Both are real, both ran against the same network at the
same time; they are simply different code paths with different bot-detection
exposure. This is exactly the "unranked, no page fetch" baseline issue #298
describes: 3 results, no ranking, snippets only, no full-page content.

--- AFTER: SearXNG search (score-ranked, sorted by search_searxng()) +
    concurrent full-page fetch of the top 5 results (RESULT_COUNT=5,
    matching WEB_SEARCH_RESULT_COUNT now set in docker-compose.yml /
    deploy-demo-box.yml), simple GET+read per page, 10 concurrent workers,
    approximating the langchain SafeWebBaseLoader default loader with
    WEB_LOADER_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS=10 ---

Query: test
  search_latency_s: 0.62
  page_fetch_latency_s (5 pages, concurrent): 0.55
  total_latency_s: 1.18
  4 of 5 pages fetched successfully (1 x HTTP 403 from a page that blocks
  non-browser user agents)

Query: canadian mortgage rates
  search_latency_s: 0.27
  page_fetch_latency_s (5 pages, concurrent): 0.45
  total_latency_s: 0.73
  3 of 5 pages fetched successfully

  NOTE: by this point in the run, google/duckduckgo/brave were already
  suspended per section 3's timeline, so bing was this query's only engine.
  Bing's own results for this specific query, and for the two below, were
  visibly off-topic under sustained load from this IP -- unrelated exam and
  news sites rather than mortgage or capital-city content. Read this as a
  second-order effect of the same anti-bot exposure (a single surviving
  engine degrading under continued load), not as a defect in this PR's
  wiring; it reinforces rather than undermines the need to confirm real
  engine health from the demo box's own (residential) egress before treating
  any specific engine's output as trustworthy in production.

